{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_riq4il7imebvtg6mf5rycl5wis67fr344qgq3ndf32pzrwlaikcq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` diff is **0 missing** symbols. All 5 C exports (`create_block`, `allocate_block`, `free_block`, `compute_hash`, `betagamma`) are exported by the Rust `.so`; every Rust import is glibc/libgcc. Nothing needed translating — the crate was already complete. Enforced as a test (`tests/symbols.rs`), not just by eyeball.\n- **Phase B** — all 43 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across ~12 000 randomized calls per configuration (fixed-seed splitmix64).\n- **Phase C** — all 10 `ERRORS.md` rows + 9 generic-boundary rows have passing differential tests asserting the *same* sentinel (`NULL`/`-1`) or the *same* signal (SIGSEGV), not merely \"both failed\".\n- **Every configuration** — 4/4 (dev × release) × (default × `--no-default-features`) via `run_all.sh`. Cargo.toml declares no `[features]`, so the script enumerates them mechanically and would pick up any addition.\n\n`cargo check` was clean from the start, and **no divergence was found** — `src/lib.rs` is unmodified.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n**1. `betagamma` is not a pure function of its arguments.** `compute_hash` branches on raw allocator *addresses*, so `betagamma`'s return value depends on heap state. Naïve in-process comparison shows \"mismatches\" that are not translation bugs — the C disagrees **with itself**: identical args gave 574 then 464 on consecutive calls, because glibc's tcache returns freed chunks LIFO and flips `mem1`/`mem2`'s address order. I test these rows by `fork()`ing two children from identical parent state, and guard the harness with C-vs-C and Rust-vs-Rust controls plus a fork-independent arithmetic cross-check.\n\n**2. I mutation-tested the suite rather than trusting green checkmarks.** 12 injected mistranslations, **11 caught**; `src/lib.rs` restored byte-identically. Three findings came out of this:\n\n- Two \"survivors\" were bugs in *my detector*, not test gaps — a divergent mutant often SIGSEGVs the test binary, which prints no `... FAILED` line. Fixed.\n- The one true survivor (`block_size` zero- vs sign-extended) is **provably indistinguishable on this host**: I measured that both the 16 GiB and the overflowing `calloc` return `NULL`. It's documented honestly with the boundary pinned by a test, rather than checked off as covered.\n- Two of my own test assertions were initially wrong, and the C corrected me: `create_block` assigns `block.flags = flags` *after* the `strcpy`, so the assignment overwrites the byte the overflow wrote; and LLVM recognizes `malloc` intrinsically and had silently deleted my memory-exhaustion loop, making the `malloc`-failure test vacuous until I routed the drain through the `.so`.\n\nScratch files removed; `c_src/` verified unmodified by checksum (only the generated `build/` was added, as instructed)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":599,"cache_read_input_tokens":165130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":599,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1b6e81d-9a5d-4938-8698-51443cbb6fae","uuid":"ae2d4e31-a0ef-4c89-b964-7ecf7484688e","timestamp":"2026-08-27T22:23:52.686Z"}
